  "summary": "<table><thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>What it enforces</th><th>What it means for your code</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Privilege levels</strong></td><td>Only the kernel can touch privileged registers and hardware.</td><td>You can't poke a peripheral directly.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Virtual memory</strong></td><td>Each process sees only its own memory.</td><td>A stray pointer faults instead of corrupting a neighbour.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>System calls</strong></td><td>One guarded entry into the kernel.</td><td>Anything outside your memory is a request, not a right.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Timer + scheduler</strong></td><td>The kernel always gets the CPU back.</td><td>No program can hog the board.</td></tr></tbody></table>",
  "breaks": "<p>Each mechanism fails in a recognisable way, and the symptom tells you which one you hit:</p>\n<ul><li><strong><code>Segmentation fault</code></strong> — you used an address with no mapping: a null or stray pointer, a freed buffer, or an attempt to reach hardware directly.</li><li><strong><code>Operation not permitted</code></strong> — a system call was refused because you lack the privilege for it, such as a device or a setting only root may use.</li><li><strong><code>Killed</code></strong> — the kernel ended the process, often because the board ran out of memory.</li><li><strong>The program hangs and uses no CPU</strong> — it is blocked inside a system call, waiting for something. Tracing the system calls, as we'll do in the demo, shows which one.</li></ul>",
  "demo": "<h4>What to watch on the serial console or SSH session</h4>\n<ol><li><strong>Watch a system call happen.</strong> Trace a trivial command such as <code>echo hi</code>. Among the lines you'll see the <code>write</code> call that carries your text to the console.</li><li><strong>Hit the wall on purpose.</strong> Run a tiny program that writes to the UART's physical address. It dies with a segmentation fault: the same line that worked on the Cortex-M.</li><li><strong>Go to the other side.</strong> Load the small kernel module, and read its message with <code>dmesg</code>. This code runs at the kernel privilege level on the same board, with no wall. A bug here would not be a segfault but a kernel crash.</li></ol>",
